    "q-132424": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132424",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the sum modulo $24$ of the solutions to the congruence $20x≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24).$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*",
      "worked_solution": "In the given congruence, we have $a=20$ and $n=24$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(20,24)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=16$ is divisible by $4,$ our congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nFirst, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20x & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,24) \\\\ 4\\cdot 5x & ≡4\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4\\cdot 6) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot 5x & ≡\\cancel{4}\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{4}\\cdot 6) \\\\ 5x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the new congruence, we have ${a}_{1}=5$ and ${n}_{1}=6$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}({a}_{1},{n}_{1})=\\text{gcd}(5,6)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $6.$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$ To find the inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ we compute the exponents of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡5\\cdot 5≡25≡4\\cdot 6+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $5\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ which means that $5$ is its own multiplicative inverse (modulo $6$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 5\\cdot 5x & ≡5\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ (5\\cdot 5)x & ≡20 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x & ≡20 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x & ≡3\\cdot 6+2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$ \n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $24$ (not modulo $6$). To find all the solutions modulo $24,$ we repeatedly add $6$ modulo $24$ until we reach the number $2$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,24) \\\\ {x}_{2} & ≡2+6≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,24) \\\\ {x}_{3} & ≡8+6≡14 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,24) \\\\ {x}_{4} & ≡14+6≡20 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,24) \\\\ {x}_{5} & ≡20+6≡26≡24+2≡2≡{x}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x≡2,8,14,20\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24).$ \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n2+8+14+20≡44≡24+20≡20\\,(\\text{mod}\\,24).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
