    "q-214162": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214162",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the solutions of $268x≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,404).$ Express your answers as nonnegative integers smaller than $404,$ and in ascending order. \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\text{gcd}(268,404)=4.$*\n\n${x}_{1}≡$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≡$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,{x}_{3}≡$\n{{field-3}}\n$,\\,{x}_{4}≡$\n{{field-4}}\n$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,404)$",
      "worked_solution": "In the given congruence, we have $a=268$ and $n=404$, and we're given that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(268,404)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=16$ is divisible by $4,$ our congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nNext, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}268x & ≡16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,404) \\\\ 4\\cdot 67x & ≡4\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4\\cdot 101) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot 67x & ≡\\cancel{4}\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{4}\\cdot 101) \\\\ 67x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in this new congruence, we have ${a}_{1}=67$ and ${n}_{1}=101$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}({a}_{1},{n}_{1})=\\text{gcd}(67,101)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $101.$\n\nTo find the solution, we must multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $67\\,(\\text{mod}\\,101).$ To find the inverse of $67\\,(\\text{mod}\\,101),$ we apply the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}101 & = & 67\\cdot 1 & + & 34\\, & ⇔ & \\,34 & = & 101-67\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  &  &  \\\\ 67 & = & 34\\cdot 1 & + & 33\\, & ⇔ & \\,33 & = & 67-34\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  &  &  \\\\ 34 & = & 33\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\, & ⇔ & \\,1 & = & 34-33\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  &  &  \\\\ 33 & = & 33\\cdot 1 & + & 0\\, &  & \\, &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, back-substituting in the usual way gives the following:\n\n$$\n1=2\\cdot 101-3\\cdot 67\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $101,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 101-3\\cdot 67 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101) \\\\ -3\\cdot 67 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101) \\\\ (101-3)\\cdot 67 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101) \\\\ 98\\cdot 67 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse of $67$ modulo $101$ is $98.$\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $98,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}67x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101) \\\\ 98\\cdot 67x & ≡98\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101) \\\\ x & ≡392 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101) \\\\ x & ≡101\\cdot 3+89 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101) \\\\ x & ≡89 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,101)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x≡89\\,(\\text{mod}\\,101).$\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $404$ (not modulo $101$). To find all the solutions modulo $404,$ we repeatedly add $101$ to $89$ modulo $404$ until we reach the number $89$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ≡89 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,404) \\\\ {x}_{2} & ≡89+101≡190 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,404) \\\\ {x}_{3} & ≡190+101≡291 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,404) \\\\ {x}_{4} & ≡291+101≡392 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,404) \\\\ {x}_{5} & ≡392+101≡493≡404+89≡{x}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,404)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1,2,3,4}≡89,\\,190,\\,291,\\,392\\,(\\text{mod}\\,404).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
